% holding-blocked: Check both bars against the plate (p. 57, scan p. 79, last staff): the lengths in bar 1 (read one eighth short) and bar 2 (read two eighths short); then read the 2nd, 3rd and 4th versions on p. 58 (scan p. 80) as variants.
% holding-kind: fragment
% holding-kind-note: The first of four versions of one tune that the men sang in turn; the 2nd to 4th (p. 58) show how they varied it. The four versions together, sung in turn, are the piece.
% dossier-function: variation-song fragment from the same men's social sing-song as Ex. 129; the four versions were sung in turn
% dossier-elements: first of four two-bar melodic variants, without words; pentatonic material and ornamental short-long figures over a G-centred open-fifth rehearsal drone
% dossier-practice: the source gives no tempo, words or exact order beyond the four numbered versions; the written E4-E5 octave is high for young unison voices, and B-flat (-2 semitones, D4-D5) is the provisional rehearsal key
% dossier-accompaniment: no drum line is printed for Ex. 130; start unaccompanied, and treat the generated G drone as our optional pitch support rather than source practice
% dossier-lyrics: no words were taken down; do not add text or infer a language
% dossier-honesty: historical-source fragment: only the first version is transcribed here; the G drone, tempo and classroom treatment are ours, and the complete four-version piece remains to be entered
% dossier-licence: public domain source (A. H. Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan, 1914; author died 1948); first version read from p. 57, with variants visible on pp. 57-58
% dossier-respect: the source records men of the 2nd Goorkhas in colonial India; do not infer a language, a pan-Nepali identity or present-day practice from the regiment or location
% dossier-review: Nepali/Gurkha culture bearer and singer, plus a second plate reader and classroom teacher; all reviews still required
% source-url: https://archive.org/details/musicofhindostan00foxs/page/57/mode/1up archive.org scan of The Music of Hindostan, at p. 57
X:1
T:Gurkha song, first version
T:Gurkha song, four versions sung in turn
C:traditional (Gurkha, Nepal)
O:India, Dharmsala (2nd Goorkhas)
R:song
S:Fox Strangways, The Music of Hindostan (1914), Ex. 130, pp. 57-58
N:From the same sing-song as Ex. 129; the plate gives four versions of the one tune, the 1st here, the 2nd to 4th on p. 58, which shows how the men varied it. No tonic is marked and there is no signature; the notes are E d e c B, a Gurkha pentatonic shape on the fourth G-c or d-G (Fox Strangways groups Exs. 129, 130 and 137 as the plagal form, p. 70); the drone is on G. No words.
N:HOW READ (2026-11-09): one staff cropped at 1200, 1600 and 2400 dpi (scratch crops w-ex130-*, n-ex130-*, d-ex130-*); pitches from the detector (E, d, e, d, d; d, B, c, c) with the first d and the first c read by eye; rhythm by eye from the 1600-dpi crop, one pass, and the bars do not add up to 4/4 as read, so the lengths here are a best fit, not a reading.
N:UNCERTAIN: every note length; the slur in bar 2 over c and d; the first d.
M:4/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=108
